  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This unit-test-only TermLike adapter records whole rendered lines.\n Indicatif's authoring renderer does not request horizontal movement,\n so this required no-op callback has no application input path.\n The authoring PTY tests exercise the real terminal implementation."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `src/authoring/progress.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[RecordingTerm/indicatif::TermLike]/fn[move_cursor_right]` is appropriate. The function is an explicit function with a body, and its commentary is fenced citation commentary, so it counts as a tracked justification. The reason is substantive and fits the current source. The function is a required no-op in a unit-test-only `TermLike` adapter (`RecordingTerm`). It is `Ok(())` and has no logic. The production code in `src/authoring/progress.rs` `BatchProgress::new` uses `console::Term::buffered_stderr()` through `ProgressDrawTarget::term_like_with_hz`, so the recorder never reaches production. The tests build a `ProgressDrawTarget::term_like` over `RecordingTerm` and assert on whole rendered lines. The commentary says the renderer does not request horizontal movement. This is plausible and matches the sibling `move_cursor_left` justification. I did not check indicatif's source for that claim. The commentary also says the PTY tests exercise the real terminal, and `tests/authoring_batch.rs` `Fixture::run_in_pty` and `Fixture::run_in_pty_with_args` do run the binary in a PTY. The citation is not redundant, because the item has no `{impl}` or `{test}` reference to the same target.",
